# Target C-II — exact outer-tooth / long-side dichotomy

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `d4351da44afc596da6e3ea9d9b473ba1145af540`  
**Status:** proved proof-only source-level reduction; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** finite Type-II statements from audited outer-tooth exactness and integer hull-route defect; low-`t` corollary additionally uses proof-only AG42  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The low-`t` branch has a very small metric outer detour but a large combinatorial outer
excess.  The existing audited tooth theorem and integer hull-route theorem convert
that mismatch into a strong physical alternative.

For consecutive loaded force-hull vertices `b_i,b_{i+1}`, let

\[
s_i=|b_i-b_{i+1}|,
\qquad
\ell_i=|W_i|
\]

where `W_i` is the distinguished outer-face unit-contact route.  Let

- `m=#\{i:s_i>1\}` be the number of nonunit loaded-hull sides;
- `u_B=x_B-m`, where `x_B=b-q` is the outer-walk excess;
- `N_2=#\{i:s_i>1,\ell_i=2\}` be the number of genuine two-edge outer teeth;
- `L_{>2}=\sum_{s_i>1}(s_i-2)_+` be total loaded-hull side length above two; and
- `d_{out}=b-per(F)=\sum_i(\ell_i-s_i)` be the metric outer detour.

Audited Section 126 proves

\[
0\le m-N_2\le u_B,
\tag{TL.1}
\]

while the integer hull-route theorem proves

\[
0\le u_B-L_{>2}\le d_{out}.
\tag{TL.2}
\]

## 1. Exact finite coupled burden

Since `x_B=m+u_B`, (TL.1) gives

\[
N_2\ge m-u_B=x_B-2u_B.
\tag{TL.3}
\]

Equation (TL.2) gives

\[
L_{>2}\ge u_B-d_{out}.
\tag{TL.4}
\]

Add (TL.3) to twice (TL.4).  The hidden integer slack cancels exactly:

\[
\boxed{
N_2+2L_{>2}
\ge x_B-2d_{out}.
}
\tag{TL.5}
\]

This is a finite source-level inequality.  It says that the combinatorial outer-walk
excess cannot disappear when metric detour is small: it must reappear either as actual
two-edge triangular teeth or as metric loaded-hull side length beyond two.

In particular

\[
\boxed{
\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
\ge {x_B-2d_{out}\over3}.
}
\tag{TL.6}
\]

The right side may of course be negative outside the low-detour regime; the useful
statement is its positive part.

## 2. Type-II normalized form

On a Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={b-per(F)\over D}.
\]

Canonical near-disk perimeter convergence gives `per(F)/D->pi`, hence

\[
{x_B\over D}=\pi+\delta-t-\kappa+o(1).
\tag{TL.7}
\]

Dividing (TL.5) by `D` gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+2L_{>2}\over D}
\ge \pi-t-\kappa-\delta.
}
\tag{TL.8}
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
\ge {\pi-t-\kappa-\delta\over3}.
}
\tag{TL.9}
\]

No assumption about a positive area or angular gap for one tooth is used.

## 3. Low-`t` elimination of `kappa+delta`

Use the same notation as the low-`t` topology/network theorem:

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\]

and

\[
R(t)={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0.
\]

Proof-only AG42 plus audited §171 gives

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t).
\]

Thus

\[
\kappa+\delta\le2R(t).
\tag{TL.10}
\]

Combining (TL.8)--(TL.10),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+2L_{>2}\over D}
\ge \pi-t-2R(t)
=5\pi+2C_0-10t.
}
\tag{TL.11}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
\ge {5\pi+2C_0-10t\over3}.
}
\tag{TL.12}
\]

This is positive throughout the present low-`t` region and well beyond it.

## 4. Numerical consequences

At

\[
t_{42}=C_{146}+0.0013751
=1.8208886379794447\ldots,
\]

(TL.11) gives

\[
\boxed{
{N_2+2L_{>2}\over D}
\ge1.3091681156103485\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{TL.13}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
\ge0.4363893718701161\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{TL.14}
\]

Even throughout the older endpoint/common-cap band `t<=1.8585`,

\[
\boxed{
\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
\ge0.3110181651349318\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{TL.15}
\]

At `t<=1.90` the same bound remains

\[
\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
\ge0.1726848318015985\ldots D-o(D).
\]

## 5. Geometric meaning of the two branches

### Tooth branch

`N_2=Omega(D)` means a linear family of nonunit loaded-hull sides is replaced by
exactly two unit contacts.  Audited Section 126 identifies these routes bijectively
with actual augmented triangular faces.  Each triangle has an unloaded apex and a
nonunit global loaded-hull base.

Moreover each tooth contributes exactly `2-s_i` to `d_out`.  Thus when `d_out/D` is
small, a large tooth family is automatically shallow in aggregate: almost all of its
bases are close to length two at any fixed tolerance.

### Long-side branch

`L_{>2}=Omega(D)` is not combinatorial bookkeeping.  It is literal Euclidean length
of consecutive loaded-hull sides beyond two.  The integer hull-route theorem also
shows

\[
0\le u_B-L_{>2}\le d_{out},
\]

so in the small-detour regime essentially all deep integer route slack is forced into
this metric long-side currency.  In addition, the rounding decomposition forces all
but `O(d_out/epsilon)` loaded side lengths to lie within `epsilon` below an integer.

Thus the second branch is a rounded long-side endpoint for the actual loaded diameter
vertices.

## 6. Frontier effect

This theorem does not close C-II, but it removes a diffuse escape route.  In the
low-`t` branch one cannot simultaneously have few two-edge teeth and little metric
length above two.  At the current frontier at least `0.436D` of one of these two
physical structures is mandatory.

The next source-level split is therefore concrete:

1. **many shallow teeth:** use the unloaded apex degree/contact geometry to force a
   large family of inward penetration contacts and couple them to the rhombic phase
   system; or
2. **large rounded long-side mass:** use the near-extremal Woodall diameter thrackle
   to constrain the angular spacing and integer-scale lengths of consecutive loaded
   hull vertices.

Neither route can be replaced by a scalar optimization over `u_B` after (TL.5).

## 7. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_outer_tooth_longside_dichotomy.py
```

for the Decimal constant checks.
